#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/id #uuid "cb816094-f468-5664-9f88-c2db4fcab4be" :question/math-academy-id "e-345" :question/problem "A farmer has $500$ meters of fencing and wants to fence off a rectangular field that borders a straight river. He needs no fence along the river. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the field with the largest area that the farmer can fence off." :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/2d81f8a2a521c0552b334dff813e37f4169d2221335ab71e391a2b0c686708ea.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA = xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+y = 500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =500 \\\\ y & =500-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = 500-2x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(500-2x) \\\\ A & =500x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $A'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 500 - 4x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 500-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =500 \\\\ x & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ x & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(x) = -4 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(125) = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $A''(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=125\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=250\\,\\textrm{m},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=375\\,\\textrm{m}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/id #uuid "5e378451-182c-5e3e-8579-f8bd8c0bbdbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-346" :question/problem "Among all rectangles with area $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2,$ what are the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP = 2(x + y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy = 25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{25}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{50}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $P'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 2 -\\frac{50}{x^2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{50}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nP''(x) = \\dfrac{100}{x^3} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad P''(5) = \\dfrac{100}{5^3} = \\dfrac 4 5 > 0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $P''(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{5}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=5\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=5\\,\\textrm{m}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $5\\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3bd584-58d2-5706-89c3-ad0d0ce8df59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.93\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355581} {:answer/id #uuid "1de19a4b-589b-58e7-9181-670e8dca2cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.72\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355582} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba2c321-786f-5ff3-ad5a-1c42780e199f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.35\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355583} {:answer/id #uuid "2c905f32-6daf-55bb-9c96-6bfed2387b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355584} {:answer/id #uuid "77cf91d1-2f7d-59b3-8b9c-61cc6d2abfb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.08\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c905f32-6daf-55bb-9c96-6bfed2387b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355584} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d6f053-48df-55c3-8bcf-7e55615e18dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1af9295c-5eff-510e-ba44-71a6e28cc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104195" :question/problem "A gardener wants to fence a rectangular flowerbed with $15$ meters of fencing. To two decimal places, what is the largest area that he can make with the fencing?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $15.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{15}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb7058b-84d3-5da2-be21-a42a427ca31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355588} {:answer/id #uuid "3e97a45f-03ce-533b-93b6-a3994bea7d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.38$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355589} {:answer/id #uuid "44b15e88-92fd-5c48-bcb3-2d83cb9647df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.56$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355590} {:answer/id #uuid "de75c14a-3934-577f-9fb7-7c1abcd6cbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.32$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355591} {:answer/id #uuid "dec4a3d4-22f4-52ff-8bc0-ef319c83c935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44b15e88-92fd-5c48-bcb3-2d83cb9647df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.56$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355590} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3954a5c-703d-5f7e-a6da-7838cdb52e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a079b2a5-f952-5f6a-93f2-9ea342f33a53" :question/math-academy-id "q-14630" :question/problem "A rectangle has a perimeter of $11$ units. What is the largest possible area of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $11.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=11.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{11}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{11}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={(\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}≈7.56.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b901ee-70ea-5318-8d9a-d788e5707fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.93$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355595} {:answer/id #uuid "20df8213-0ce5-511a-bdd1-0077771a66c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.46$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355596} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f6f164-adc2-5fc0-8dec-4e6bfce1f287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355597} {:answer/id #uuid "08c9e1be-96b7-536b-a32f-688760c56731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13.86$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355598} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fa1ec6-4ed2-5ab0-98ae-5499d80c77e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c9e1be-96b7-536b-a32f-688760c56731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13.86$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355598} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d1cf1f-6b9a-5c21-8008-122108fb4ea8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2039ef62-539b-53d1-9128-d53416c47d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14637" :question/problem "A rectangle has an area of $12$ square units. What is the minimum possible perimeter of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $12.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{12}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{24}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{48}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{48}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{3}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=2\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, the smallest possible perimeter is\n\n$$\nP=4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}≈13.86\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b265fb1-324a-59c1-9d6d-a3491b9c0442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098799} {:answer/id #uuid "efbf201b-3e65-5d5f-bb32-a60deb1d5047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098800} {:answer/id #uuid "224fe54f-7818-5175-994a-0728757f71ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098801} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae1e93-ad32-50c5-904a-5a6f297cf9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098802} {:answer/id #uuid "6767a21b-750d-59d4-a9d8-c0e52a3bf487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b265fb1-324a-59c1-9d6d-a3491b9c0442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098799} :answer-field/id #uuid "3052d6a0-a35c-5349-875a-6b8691bb4343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "566640e7-e553-5ccd-97d8-8a21319d9a2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17810" :question/problem "A builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/275803f7f72469a8427b137b5e8523475cc0605f033bedaa2280a5d28f453c38.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $320\\,\\text{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=320.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =320 \\\\ x & =320-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=320-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(320-2y)y \\\\ A & =320y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=320-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 320-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =320 \\\\ y & =\\frac{320}{4} \\\\ y & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=80$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(80)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(80)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=80$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=80$, we have \n\n$$\nx=320-2(80)=160.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=160$ and $y=80,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=240.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd4fc09b-a1f6-558d-81c1-4761e11d87ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,875\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355602} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8bfea8-0a20-5486-91a9-7d8485688e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56\\,875\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355603} {:answer/id #uuid "d15095a6-96c6-57ed-af5c-f3bf0eea192f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355604} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5a2194-fe91-5c79-a370-cff18bc8274f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,500\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355605} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c0c45a-0716-525b-8134-0ef1fe073d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1c0c45a-0716-525b-8134-0ef1fe073d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355606} :answer-field/id #uuid "2044b0dd-ff1a-5da5-8a40-c8a4fe74c7ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bc8e5e4-b288-5a94-8582-039083fac2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-3462" :question/problem "A farmer needs to enclose a field with a rectangular fence. He has $500\\,\\text{ft}$ of fencing material. A large building is on one side of the field so this side will not require any fencing. Determine the largest possible area that can be enclosed by the rectangular fence." :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/050111089f76ecb6d2b278fce0d1faa71635bc07d107d2539cf5f4bb28a32476.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\text{ft}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =500 \\\\ x & =500-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=500-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(500-2y)y \\\\ A & =500y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=500-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 500-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =500 \\\\ y & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ y & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(125)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=250$ and $y=125,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=250\\cdot 125=31\\,250.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "361ff4ea-4bf0-5084-ae3e-e6b810cba5cd" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Area of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b2d599-1f76-551d-8c66-2be7efadbfe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355609} {:answer/id #uuid "7708e797-3eb7-52e0-978c-1957f6d7baf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b2d599-1f76-551d-8c66-2be7efadbfe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355609} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf7d6fca-bbc8-5c99-88ef-f0c2723b1a40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c18e91d-d115-5092-8f35-62d57d9f04fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-346240" :question/problem "A contractor has $18\\,in$ of trim to mark out a rectangular opening. Find the maximum area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that can be marked out with the trim. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,{in}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectagular area.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/59c6b4e09015898c1cd77d96fba2bc0dca037ef7462a15f90765257888e3331d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $18.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =18 \\\\ y & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=9-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(9-x) \\\\ A & =9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=9-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{9}{2}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=9-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in$ and $y=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{max}}=xy=\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=20.25\\,{in}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953991a9-70a9-5834-b6ae-30636412f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "953991a9-70a9-5834-b6ae-30636412f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355613} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb25f03-2df0-5878-bc97-6790ba179943" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bfca220-e743-513a-a7b9-9f2e7c7ce893" :question/math-academy-id "q-346249" :question/problem "A rectangle has an area of $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cd9fa71ea72045881c25c80de4f6d8b294b60d92ea4e4f27170d68849ce31431.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=450.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{450}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{900}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =450 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{1800}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1800}{(15\\sqrt{2})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{15\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{2}}=15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{2}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2270664b-0fae-5f97-a67b-0bf99c5b2615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355616} {:answer/id #uuid "493d472a-a997-5bb8-b0ee-4554c64e2d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "493d472a-a997-5bb8-b0ee-4554c64e2d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355617} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a242ce2-ae2b-555d-a1e6-621cd3d9920b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34dba710-f1cf-51fd-96bd-5f5055ccd509" :question/math-academy-id "q-346253" :question/problem "A rectangle has an area of $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cf3d29b794698ff529da4c21f6fd02b3487d1650ec84b3c1f992280616f7701d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=675.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{675}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1350}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1350 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =675 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2700}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{2700}{(15\\sqrt{3})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{15\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{45}{\\sqrt{3}}=15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{3}\\,ft.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{3}=60\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5ecff7e-5560-524f-9424-34a07b9cb873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5ecff7e-5560-524f-9424-34a07b9cb873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355620} :answer-field/id #uuid "a186e863-f8b8-50bc-bdb8-9f472aa18960" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd7f1e01-e331-5677-8075-e459da6f04c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-346255" :question/problem "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b1e86b-1e99-5771-abb1-6cac5dfbe1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Perimeter of a Rectangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acdfa068-ef56-50a8-a512-42ad6bfa77f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355623} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6b2ae2-0871-5e0f-ada2-847b82aa85ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acdfa068-ef56-50a8-a512-42ad6bfa77f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355623} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d24ab8e-28a2-5dd5-907d-ec75fd97ee99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ea23817-6b07-5df5-a2da-49a9ad877cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-346256" :question/problem "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$"}]}
